   accepted (`{ published: true }`, 5 run-issue instances materialized), but
   after the runner's startup catch-up nothing is consumed live; two runner
   restarts did not advance instances, and the third restart returned an empty
   instance list (KV vs prisma store split). Instance-level terminality is
   proved deterministically instead: the saga tests drive the frozen
   definitions' own handlers (forward path → `sagaComplete`; money-lost →
   `sagaFail`; redeliveries no-ops), and the persisted-state shape was verified
   in the engine source — the compensator never persists state, so `.on()` is
   the only path that terminalises (Saga fix 1/3, commit 9d569e4).

### What "terminal" means here, mechanically

The undo is executed by the effector (durable, retried, keyed
`refund:${runItemId}`), the run item is stamped COMPENSATED in the same
transaction that folds it into `failedCount`, and the saga instance is
terminalised from `.on('RunItemChargeRecordFailed')` via `sagaFail` — the
engine persists `failed`. The instance can never rest `compensating`: the
compensation handler is registered as a routing no-op and the `.on()` handler
guards replay. Zero double-refund is a property of the keys, not of anyone
behaving: the refund key replays a stored REFUNDED response.
